This hands-on exercise provides a practical implementation of geospatial predictive modelling using the geographical random forest method. Building upon the fundamental concept that event occurrences are neither uniform nor random in spatial distribution, this exercise will guide you through correlating historical geospatial locations with environmental factors that represent spatial constraints and influences.
The workflow encompasses preparing training and test data sets using appropriate spatial sampling methods, calibrating predictive models through both geospatial statistical learning and machine learning approaches, performing model comparison and selection to identify the optimal predictive model, and ultimately generating future outcome predictions using the best-performing calibrated model. This comprehensive process demonstrates how known outcomes and predictors can be utilized to develop predictive capabilities for future events within a geospatial context.


In this hands-on exercise, predictive modelling will be implemented by using the geographical random forest method.
The process involves:

preparing training and test data sets by using appropriate data sampling methods;
calibrating predictive models by using geospatial statistical learning and machine learning methods;
comparing and selecting the best model for predicting the future outcome;
predicting the future outcomes by using the best calibrated model.

